FIXED STORY — USE THIS EXACT STORY
The story below is already chosen. Do not pitch 10 alternatives and do not ask me to choose another idea. Step 0 should only perform the connection test. In Step 1, use this complete narration as the source script. Keep the three planted clues [P1], [P2], [P3] and their exact payoffs [P1>], [P2>], [P3>]. Do not invent extra planted clues. You may divide long sentences into shot-sized lines, but do not remove story events or change the ending. The narration is approximately 737 words; keep the FULL film within the requested 2:00–2:45 target by checking the actual spoken-word timing and clearly report any timing conflict rather than silently cutting the story.

In my town, nobody remembers tomorrow.

You see, every night at 11:59, the clock tower rings once, and every person forgets what they planned to do the next day.

Everyone loves it here.

The baker writes tomorrow's bread recipe on a note and pins it to his door.

The teacher leaves lessons on her desk so she can remember what to teach.

The fishermen tie little blue strings around their wrists to remind themselves to go to the harbor.

And every evening, the whole town gathers in the square to watch the clock.

They call it the Fresh Start.

My mother says it makes every morning feel like a new life.

My grandfather says the tower has protected us for as long as anyone can remember.

[P1] Grandfather always tells me: never let the clock stop.

[P2] But I notice the clock has thirteen numbers, and nobody else seems to see the last one.

[P3] Grandfather has one strange saying: "Tomorrow is not lost. It is waiting."

I am the only child who remembers yesterday's plans.

At first, I think I am special.

I remember the names of people who leave town.

I remember promises people make.

I even remember a birthday party that nobody else remembers having.

Then one evening, I find a folded note beneath my bed.

The handwriting is mine, but the paper is yellow and brittle.

It says: "Noah, if the train arrives, do not let it reach the station. And whatever happens, do not let the clock strike twelve."

I run to Grandfather, but he only looks at the note and turns pale.

Before he can explain, a whistle echoes from beyond the hills.

A black train rolls into town, even though our railway has been abandoned for fifty years.

The doors open.

People step down wearing old-fashioned clothes.

Some are carrying toys, some hold flowers, and one little girl is holding a photograph of me.

The townspeople cheer.

They say the visitors are tomorrow's guests, finally arriving early.

But I recognize one of them.

It is the baker's daughter, who disappeared when I was little.

She looks exactly the same.

She whispers, "Noah, you have to remember us."

The station clock begins to move.

11:57.

The crowd dances around the train.

11:58.

The visitors start fading whenever the clock ticks.

11:59.

I finally understand.

The tower is not stealing tomorrow's memories.

It is trapping every tomorrow that the town refuses to reach.

The train carries all the days that were erased, and the people inside are the lives we were supposed to live.

I look up at the tower.

The thirteenth number is glowing.

The old note in my hand suddenly changes.

A new sentence appears: "The clock cannot count what has already been given away."

I remember the blue strings the fishermen use.

I remember the baker's notes.

I remember every little promise people leave behind because they know they will forget.

I race through the square, gathering the notes from doors, desks, windows and pockets.

The crowd shouts.

Grandfather tries to reach me, but he remembers the rule and freezes.

I climb the station signal platform and tie every note together with the blue strings.

Then I pull the long chain across the railway tracks.

The train hits the paper chain, but the strange light catches every written promise.

Thousands of tiny words rise into the air.

The clock tower shudders.

Its hands move past midnight.

For the first time, the bell does not ring.

Every person in town remembers.

The baker remembers his daughter.

The teacher remembers the students who grew up.

My mother remembers the name she gave me before the Fresh Start.

And Grandfather remembers why he warned me.

[P1>] "Never let the clock stop" was not a warning to obey the tower. It was a warning that someone had to keep time moving.

[P2>] The thirteenth number was not an hour. It was the count of all the tomorrows trapped inside.

[P3>] "Tomorrow is not lost. It is waiting" was the truth.

The train doors open again.

The visitors step onto the platform, and the town finally gets its tomorrows back.

I think it is over.

Then the station lights turn blue.

A second train appears on the hill.

Its front carries a date from one hundred years before our town was built.

And from inside, someone who looks exactly like me taps on the window.

